265
00:10:49.020 --> 00:10:51.810
I often obviously see that a lot in maybe religion,

266
00:10:51.810 --> 00:10:53.010
but a lot of self-help books.

267
00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:56.100
It's like, these are the 10 steps to solving your issue,

268
00:10:56.100 --> 00:10:57.453
whatever issue X is.

269
00:10:58.290 --> 00:11:00.540
So first and foremost, the reason it's a mindset

270
00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:02.850
is because I wanted to find a way

271
00:11:02.850 --> 00:11:06.210
that allow people who read the book or to practice

272
00:11:06.210 --> 00:11:09.390
and be part of, kind of bringing Longpath into their life

273
00:11:09.390 --> 00:11:12.720
to not feel that it was overly prescriptive, or dogmatic,

274
00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:14.361
or this is the way you have to do it

275
00:11:14.361 --> 00:11:15.990
to see the results that you want.

276
00:11:15.990 --> 00:11:18.330
It was more like, here's how you activate

277
00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:21.570
and live in this moment so that you can navigate it

278
00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:22.680
in a positive sense.

279
00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:25.920
So that's what Longpath is, it's a mindset.

280
00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:27.030
Pure and simple.

281
00:11:27.030 --> 00:11:30.060
But specifically it's a mindset to navigate this moment.

282
00:11:30.060 --> 00:11:31.440
So what I talk about in the book

283
00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:34.173
is that we're in this intertidal phase.

284
00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:37.727
So the way to think about an intertidal,

285
00:11:37.727 --> 00:11:40.530
when you go to the intertidal zone in an ocean,

286
00:11:40.530 --> 00:11:42.270
obviously I use it as a metaphor,

287
00:11:42.270 --> 00:11:46.050
it's that piece of land that is always either above water

288
00:11:46.050 --> 00:11:48.270
or below water based on the tides.

289
00:11:48.270 --> 00:11:50.730
And in the intertidal, you kind of have

290
00:11:50.730 --> 00:11:54.990
both this massive chaos, it's a very disruptive zone,

291
00:11:54.990 --> 00:11:56.280
but also massive creativity.

292
00:11:56.280 --> 00:11:58.140
You have muscles that learn how to keep water

293
00:11:58.140 --> 00:12:01.320
so they don't suffocate and all these other things.

294
00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:04.650
But intertidal, in some ways kind of like an inter regnant,

295
00:12:04.650 --> 00:12:06.150
what Graham, she used to talk about,

296
00:12:06.150 --> 00:12:08.370
which is kind of the old ways are dying

297
00:12:08.370 --> 00:12:10.200
and the new ones are yet to be born.

298
00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:13.430
So I realize in the work that I've been doing

299
00:12:13.430 --> 00:12:16.500
at Longpath Labs and previous work for 20 years,

300
00:12:16.500 --> 00:12:18.570
that we were in this intertidal moment

301
00:12:18.570 --> 00:12:20.490
as a global civilization.

302
00:12:20.490 --> 00:12:25.290
And so if we want to kind of positively navigate it,

303
00:12:25.290 --> 00:12:26.700
we're gonna need a mindset

304
00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:28.530
that's kind of bespoke to the moment.

305
00:12:28.530 --> 00:12:33.240
And so Longpath is, sure maybe 40 or 50 years ago

306
00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:35.280
could have been an interesting mindset,

307
00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:37.813
but I really kind of created and did the research for it

308
00:12:37.813 --> 00:12:39.810
so that it could be used right now

309
00:12:39.810 --> 00:12:41.220
in this intertidal moment.

310
00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:45.210
So essentially it's a mindset, it's an active verb,

311
00:12:45.210 --> 00:12:49.620
it's an approach to living, thinking, and problem solving

312
00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:54.090
that considers not just future generations

313
00:12:54.090 --> 00:12:56.460
and the implications of our actions now

314
00:12:56.460 --> 00:13:00.510
on a future that has yet to be written,

315
00:13:00.510 --> 00:13:05.510
but also importantly has to consider the past as well.

316
00:13:05.790 --> 00:13:07.770
Like there is this, in the book,

317
00:13:07.770 --> 00:13:10.530
you talk a lot about like how we can't solve these problems

318
00:13:10.530 --> 00:13:12.450
and think about the future we want

319
00:13:12.450 --> 00:13:15.090
without really contemplating and understanding the past

320
00:13:15.090 --> 00:13:17.850
from which we come from, our ancestry.

321
00:13:17.850 --> 00:13:20.760
Yeah, I mean, look, so your previous question is like,

322
00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:21.660
how did I get to this?

323
00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:23.973
Like how did I come to Longpath?

324
00:13:25.410 --> 00:13:28.560
Everyone has an interesting story, an origin story

325
00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:29.490
of how they got there.

326
00:13:29.490 --> 00:13:32.040
Mine starts off, unlike most people when they talk about

327
00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:33.660
their story they're like, well, I was born in this year,

328
00:13:33.660 --> 00:13:34.890
in this location.

329
00:13:34.890 --> 00:13:39.060
Mine starts off in the 1920s when my father is born

330
00:13:39.060 --> 00:13:43.200
in a small town in Poland, kind of a schtatle if you will.

331
00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:45.870
It was Poland that it's now Belarus.

332
00:13:45.870 --> 00:13:50.850
And he was 17 years old when Germany invaded Poland.

333
00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:52.110
And within the first seven days

334
00:13:52.110 --> 00:13:55.320
he lost his two older brothers to the Russian front.

335
00:13:55.320 --> 00:13:59.520
And within months, all the Jews had been kind of put

336
00:13:59.520 --> 00:14:00.480
into the Jewish ghetto.

337
00:14:00.480 --> 00:14:02.490
So it was him, his father, his younger brother,

338
00:14:02.490 --> 00:14:04.080
mother and sister.

339
00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:06.180
And within several months,

340
00:14:06.180 --> 00:14:07.950
the mother and sister had been shipped off

341
00:14:07.950 --> 00:14:08.783
to a forced labor camp.

342
00:14:08.783 --> 00:14:11.523
We later found out they had been sent to Auschwitz.

343
00:14:12.570 --> 00:14:17.570
And so who was left was my father, his brother and his dad.

344
00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:23.250
And at one point the Germans as a kind of sick and twisted,

345
00:14:23.250 --> 00:14:24.810
I don't know what game,

346
00:14:24.810 --> 00:14:26.520
decided it'd be interesting to see what would happen

347
00:14:26.520 --> 00:14:29.100
if they shot my grandfather in front of my dad,

348
00:14:29.100 --> 00:14:31.710
but told my dad that if he'd lean down to help him

349
00:14:31.710 --> 00:14:32.940
he'd be next.

350
00:14:32.940 --> 00:14:35.040
Would he actually kind of break or not.

351
00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:36.450
So awful.

352
00:14:36.450 --> 00:14:41.450
And so clearly my dad didn't lean down because I'm here

353
00:14:41.790 --> 00:14:46.790
but soon thereafter, he and his brother escaped the ghetto

354
00:14:47.100 --> 00:14:48.540
and they joined the Jewish underground

355
00:14:48.540 --> 00:14:50.220
and they joined the resistance.

356
00:14:50.220 --> 00:14:52.020
So for about two and a half, three years,

357
00:14:52.020 --> 00:14:53.190
he was part of the Jewish underground,

358
00:14:53.190 --> 00:14:55.110
rose to become kind of a commander.

359
00:14:55.110 --> 00:14:57.000
And soon after the war became a Nazi hunter

360
00:14:57.000 --> 00:14:58.407
and there's a whole story there.

361
00:14:58.407 --> 00:15:00.480
I mean, what a ballsy thing to do,

362
00:15:00.480 --> 00:15:03.030
'cause most people would've just fled, right?

363
00:15:03.030 --> 00:15:04.680
Most people would've just fled.

364
00:15:05.952 --> 00:15:07.530
The story that I'm telling you is one

365
00:15:07.530 --> 00:15:09.930
of the first stories I remember being told.

366
00:15:09.930 --> 00:15:12.540
This is where I learned the idea of agency

367
00:15:12.540 --> 00:15:14.220
and where you can actually make a difference,

368
00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:15.150
even if it's small.

369
00:15:15.150 --> 00:15:18.090
People often asked him, why didn't you flee?

370
00:15:18.090 --> 00:15:20.730
And his reply is somewhat grim,

371
00:15:20.730 --> 00:15:23.430
but it was basically like, every Nazi I can kill

372
00:15:23.430 --> 00:15:25.860
is one less Nazi that can kill us.

373
00:15:25.860 --> 00:15:26.910
That was it.

374
00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:29.430
It was visceral and it was real

375
00:15:29.430 --> 00:15:30.783
but that was his reality.

376
00:15:32.340 --> 00:15:36.510
So after the war, he was arrested by the Americans

377
00:15:36.510 --> 00:15:38.190
and placed into like an IDP camp

378
00:15:38.190 --> 00:15:42.060
because he was smuggling Czech orphans to Palestine

379
00:15:42.060 --> 00:15:44.526
or pre Israel right to the Palestinian mandate.

380
00:15:44.526 --> 00:15:47.790
And they arrested him, and he is kinda given a choice,

381
00:15:47.790 --> 00:15:49.140
either work with the Americans

382
00:15:49.140 --> 00:15:52.860
or work with the Russians doing more intelligence work,

383
00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:56.824
or basically stay in his IDP camp for who knows how long.

384
00:15:56.824 --> 00:15:57.870
And what is he like?

385
00:15:57.870 --> 00:15:59.700
22 or something like that at this time.

386
00:15:59.700 --> 00:16:00.813
Yeah, and so what he eventually does is,

387
00:16:00.813 --> 00:16:02.100
with the help of some cousins,

388
00:16:02.100 --> 00:16:05.614
he escapes and that ends up becoming...

389
00:16:05.614 --> 00:16:08.100
Basically, post-war Europe was a mess.

390
00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:10.830
So he would go back and forth between racing motorcycles

391
00:16:10.830 --> 00:16:13.620
on the Autobon and playing semi-pro soccer.

392
00:16:13.620 --> 00:16:18.620
And eventually he and his brother through Portugal decide

393
00:16:19.560 --> 00:16:20.670
they wanna go to America.

394
00:16:20.670 --> 00:16:24.630
To him, Europe is dead, Europe is a graveyard

395
00:16:24.630 --> 00:16:27.180
both of his family and everything he's ever known.

396
00:16:27.180 --> 00:16:29.400
And besides America is where he wants to go,

397
00:16:29.400 --> 00:16:31.770
but at the time America's not letting any Jews in.

398
00:16:31.770 --> 00:16:33.060
So the only place that would let him in

399
00:16:33.060 --> 00:16:34.560
in that general direction was Cuba.

400
00:16:34.560 --> 00:16:37.440
So he lands in Cuba in 1952.

401
00:16:37.440 --> 00:16:39.390
And soon after he's in a...

402
00:16:39.390 --> 00:16:41.790
This is obviously pre Castro, Cuba.

403
00:16:41.790 --> 00:16:45.390
He is in a casino and there's this old man behind him

404
00:16:45.390 --> 00:16:48.480
sitting on the couches kind of whistling a tune,

405
00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:50.880
but it's an old Yiddish tune from the old country.

406
00:16:50.880 --> 00:16:53.490
So my dad kind of like whistles the tune with him.

407
00:16:53.490 --> 00:16:54.630
The guy, oh, they start talking.

408
00:16:54.630 --> 00:16:56.900
Well, that guy ends up being Meyer Lansky.

409
00:16:56.900 --> 00:16:59.763
So if you know, Meyer..

410
00:16:59.763 --> 00:17:01.906
Your dad's life is a movie.

411
00:17:01.906 --> 00:17:03.600
This is the movie I wanna see.

412
00:17:03.600 --> 00:17:05.250
So I'm giving you the...

413
00:17:05.250 --> 00:17:06.810
You're gonna see this kind of convergence.

414
00:17:06.810 --> 00:17:09.120
So he's doing this for about 10 years

415
00:17:09.120 --> 00:17:10.260
through the revolution.

416
00:17:10.260 --> 00:17:11.610
And eventually, we don't know the full story,

417
00:17:11.610 --> 00:17:13.530
but he and caster have some sort of falling out

418
00:17:13.530 --> 00:17:16.170
and they show up at my dad's factory one day and said,

419
00:17:16.170 --> 00:17:17.003
you gotta go, you gotta go.

420
00:17:17.003 --> 00:17:19.420
But that presupposes that they developed a relationship

421
00:17:19.420 --> 00:17:21.487
of some sort of at some point--

422
00:17:21.487 --> 00:17:22.710
Of some sort.
... around something.

423
00:17:22.710 --> 00:17:23.543
Around something.

424
00:17:23.543 --> 00:17:25.520
So your dad becomes this like industrialist, right?

425
00:17:25.520 --> 00:17:27.630
Not the industrialist, but he's one of the few people

426
00:17:27.630 --> 00:17:30.600
in Cuba that time who was a fluent speaker in Spanish

427
00:17:30.600 --> 00:17:33.519
and Russian who had been in Cuba--

428
00:17:33.519 --> 00:17:35.160
That makes it very valuable to Castro.

429
00:17:35.160 --> 00:17:36.360
Very valuable.

430
00:17:36.360 --> 00:17:38.310
Valuable enough and friendly enough

431
00:17:38.310 --> 00:17:39.930
that they kicked him out which was great,

432
00:17:39.930 --> 00:17:42.060
'cause a lot of people did not get that option.

433
00:17:42.060 --> 00:17:43.200
They were just--

434
00:17:43.200 --> 00:17:46.830
If you had to guess, what do you imagine the falling out

435
00:17:46.830 --> 00:17:48.093
with Castro was about?

436
00:17:49.320 --> 00:17:50.247
I'm sure you have theories on that.

437
00:17:50.247 --> 00:17:52.200
But yeah, the theories it has to do with him

438
00:17:52.200 --> 00:17:53.790
dating Castro's sister and other stuff like that.

439
00:17:53.790 --> 00:17:56.067
Those are the--
The plot thickens.

440
00:17:56.067 --> 00:17:58.920
I mean, knowing my dad as my dad.

441
00:17:58.920 --> 00:18:01.650
And so there was something.

442
00:18:01.650 --> 00:18:04.320
So Mexico wouldn't accept you

443
00:18:04.320 --> 00:18:05.730
if you didn't have a round trip ticket.

444
00:18:05.730 --> 00:18:08.040
So for many years we had the return ticket

445
00:18:08.040 --> 00:18:09.630
from Mexico city back to Havana.

446
00:18:09.630 --> 00:18:10.980
But he was told, never come back,

447
00:18:10.980 --> 00:18:12.843
but he kept that return ticket.

448
00:18:14.250 --> 00:18:15.083
Were you born?

449
00:18:15.083 --> 00:18:16.135
You were born in Mexico.

450
00:18:16.135 --> 00:18:17.250
I was born in Mexico.

451
00:18:17.250 --> 00:18:19.740
So we're gonna pause on that story, and it's the mid '60s.

452
00:18:19.740 --> 00:18:21.750
Meanwhile, so my dad's born in the '20s,

453
00:18:21.750 --> 00:18:23.470
my mom is born in 1945

454
00:18:24.510 --> 00:18:28.050
and she's kind of a radical artist student

455
00:18:28.050 --> 00:18:29.310
in San Francisco Bay Area.

456
00:18:29.310 --> 00:18:31.890
And she's working with Bill Graham and Janice Joplin

457
00:18:31.890 --> 00:18:34.290
and doing all the posters for the Fillmore.

458
00:18:34.290 --> 00:18:39.290
And she starts studying with an architect, an engineer

459
00:18:40.560 --> 00:18:42.960
that's known but not totally known

460
00:18:42.960 --> 00:18:44.550
by the name of Buckminster Fuller.

461
00:18:44.550 --> 00:18:47.340
So Bucky becomes my mom's teacher.

462
00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:48.173
That's so nice.

463
00:18:48.173 --> 00:18:49.530
And so she ends up studying a bunch of time

464
00:18:49.530 --> 00:18:50.363
with Buckminster Fuller.

465
00:18:50.363 --> 00:18:51.196
So you're starting to see,

466
00:18:51.196 --> 00:18:52.530
you're asking where Longpath came from,

467
00:18:52.530 --> 00:18:54.570
but there's an etymology here.

468
00:18:54.570 --> 00:18:55.773
Sure, now I get it.

469
00:18:57.210 --> 00:18:58.920
And so spending a lot of time with Bucky,

470
00:18:58.920 --> 00:19:02.730
he kind of helps her see the world in very different ways

471
00:19:02.730 --> 00:19:04.140
and kind of thinking.

472
00:19:04.140 --> 00:19:05.700
But what he eventually says is he goes,

473
00:19:05.700 --> 00:19:09.690
look, you're only gonna go get so far in your thinking

474
00:19:09.690 --> 00:19:12.060
if you stay within this kind of American context,

475
00:19:12.060 --> 00:19:13.050
you need to go abroad.

476
00:19:13.050 --> 00:19:14.280
But this is like the '60s.

477
00:19:14.280 --> 00:19:15.720
It's not like today where you can jump on a plane

478
00:19:15.720 --> 00:19:16.553
and go anywhere.

479
00:19:16.553 --> 00:19:19.470
So the furthest her parents would let her go was Mexico.

480
00:19:19.470 --> 00:19:22.080
So my mom ends up in Mexico,

481
00:19:22.080 --> 00:19:25.080
20 year difference between my dad and her.

482
00:19:25.080 --> 00:19:28.020
They get set up on a blind Shabbat date and they're married.

483
00:19:28.020 --> 00:19:32.010
So you have this kind of Nazi hunting, Antifa,

484
00:19:32.010 --> 00:19:35.730
anti-totalitarian, capitalist industrialist

485
00:19:35.730 --> 00:19:40.590
with this kind of artsy, Bucky, younger American.

486
00:19:40.590 --> 00:19:42.120
And myself and my children, sisters are born

487
00:19:42.120 --> 00:19:43.500
in Guadalajara in the '70s.

488
00:19:43.500 --> 00:19:45.360
Yeah, unbelievable.

489
00:19:45.360 --> 00:19:48.480
And when you trace that etymology in the kind of strains

490
00:19:48.480 --> 00:19:52.650
of thought, the polarity between your parents,

491
00:19:52.650 --> 00:19:56.130
you can extract from each of those individuals aspects

492
00:19:56.130 --> 00:20:00.990
that work kind of mend fluidly to create the person

493
00:20:00.990 --> 00:20:01.950
that you are today.

494
00:20:01.950 --> 00:20:04.590
'Cause it's this combination of being somebody

495
00:20:04.590 --> 00:20:06.510
who really understands systems

496
00:20:06.510 --> 00:20:10.710
and how to move entities, corporations, individuals,

497
00:20:10.710 --> 00:20:14.490
political organizations forward in a progressive way,

498
00:20:14.490 --> 00:20:16.920
but also this flare for creativity

499
00:20:16.920 --> 00:20:18.810
and out of the box thinking.

500
00:20:18.810 --> 00:20:20.280
Yeah, and the funny thing is when you're saying that,

501
00:20:20.280 --> 00:20:21.838
I didn't know which parent you were talking about.

502
00:20:21.838 --> 00:20:23.550
(Swan laughs)

503
00:20:23.550 --> 00:20:24.960
Because there's a little bit of both of them.

504
00:20:24.960 --> 00:20:25.793
My dad--

505
00:20:25.793 --> 00:20:28.260
Well, I was thinking the systems comes from the dad.

506
00:20:28.260 --> 00:20:30.000
Yeah, but Bucky was also a system.

507
00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:30.833
Yeah, is as true, right?

508
00:20:30.833 --> 00:20:33.510
My mom always saw the world in systems.

509
00:20:33.510 --> 00:20:34.872
And so it's interesting,

510
00:20:34.872 --> 00:20:36.600
it was a little bit of both in both of them,

511
00:20:36.600 --> 00:20:38.340
obviously enough that there was an overlap.

512
00:20:38.340 --> 00:20:42.090
It wasn't vinegar, oil, water.

513
00:20:42.090 --> 00:20:42.923
It was like there was something there,

514
00:20:42.923 --> 00:20:44.830
but there was enough overlap, but 100%.

515
00:20:44.830 --> 00:20:47.980
Yeah, but also the shared DNA being this

516
00:20:48.899 --> 00:20:52.950
kind of thinking that's untethered from tradition

517
00:20:52.950 --> 00:20:55.680
or expectation to imagine a better future.

518
00:20:55.680 --> 00:20:56.513
100%.

519
00:20:56.513 --> 00:20:57.390
I mean, this was...

520
00:20:57.390 --> 00:21:00.050
When my dad finally became an American citizen

521
00:21:00.050 --> 00:21:02.430
in '80s, he was like, this is it, like I'm done.

522
00:21:02.430 --> 00:21:04.170
And we were like, well, what do you mean you're done?

523
00:21:04.170 --> 00:21:07.350
He was like, this was my revenge on Hitler.

524
00:21:07.350 --> 00:21:09.690
My revenge on Hitler was getting to a free country

525
00:21:09.690 --> 00:21:10.650
and having children

