1209
00:49:13.950 --> 00:49:15.466
like here's what you can do.

1210
00:49:15.466 --> 00:49:18.360
And that's a very Western modality, right?

1211
00:49:18.360 --> 00:49:20.100
It's a very kind of truncated.

1212
00:49:20.100 --> 00:49:21.240
It's all about you.

1213
00:49:21.240 --> 00:49:24.810
It's a very kind of no offense to anyone,

1214
00:49:24.810 --> 00:49:26.730
but it's a very kind of almost narcissistic way

1215
00:49:26.730 --> 00:49:29.040
of thinking about your role in the world

1216
00:49:29.040 --> 00:49:30.720
is that it's just from your birth to your death.

1217
00:49:30.720 --> 00:49:32.730
And it discounts what came before you

1218
00:49:32.730 --> 00:49:34.740
and what's gonna come after you.

1219
00:49:34.740 --> 00:49:38.820
So transgenerational empathy asks you to look back

1220
00:49:38.820 --> 00:49:41.460
at your parents, and your grandparents,

1221
00:49:41.460 --> 00:49:42.540
and your great grandparents,

1222
00:49:42.540 --> 00:49:44.340
at an individual level

1223
00:49:44.340 --> 00:49:48.780
and understand that what they did shaped who you are today

1224
00:49:48.780 --> 00:49:50.820
for better or for worse.

1225
00:49:50.820 --> 00:49:52.050
And at the core of it,

1226
00:49:52.050 --> 00:49:54.450
and the reason why it's not transgenerational thinking,

1227
00:49:54.450 --> 00:49:57.900
but empathy is so that you can connect with them

1228
00:49:57.900 --> 00:50:00.870
and understand what they were going through,

1229
00:50:00.870 --> 00:50:02.760
why did they make those decisions,

1230
00:50:02.760 --> 00:50:04.557
and how is that impacting you?

1231
00:50:04.557 --> 00:50:06.480
Now that doesn't mean you let them off the hook.

1232
00:50:06.480 --> 00:50:08.160
If your parents did something terrible,

1233
00:50:08.160 --> 00:50:09.330
transitional empathy doesn't mean,

1234
00:50:09.330 --> 00:50:10.590
well, I absolve you.

1235
00:50:10.590 --> 00:50:12.960
But it means it means having a conversation,

1236
00:50:12.960 --> 00:50:14.698
if they're alive or if they're not

1237
00:50:14.698 --> 00:50:16.230
and investigating that.

1238
00:50:16.230 --> 00:50:19.980
It's a kind of, it's looking at the emotional inheritance

1239
00:50:19.980 --> 00:50:22.010
that you received from those who came before you

1240
00:50:22.010 --> 00:50:23.160
at an individual level,

1241
00:50:23.160 --> 00:50:25.110
but also a societal level

1242
00:50:25.110 --> 00:50:28.470
and saying, okay, I understand that

1243
00:50:28.470 --> 00:50:31.410
that is present in me in these certain ways,

1244
00:50:31.410 --> 00:50:33.690
but it doesn't have to define who I am.

1245
00:50:33.690 --> 00:50:36.210
So if we go back to the story of my father, yeah.

1246
00:50:36.210 --> 00:50:37.320
Anyone listening to this is like,

1247
00:50:37.320 --> 00:50:40.060
oh, that dude's dad definitely had PTSD

1248
00:50:41.040 --> 00:50:42.360
undiagnosed and untreated

1249
00:50:42.360 --> 00:50:44.640
and that definitely manifests in Ari's life

1250
00:50:44.640 --> 00:50:46.170
with his anxiety and the way he looks

1251
00:50:46.170 --> 00:50:47.760
at the world, da, da, da.

1252
00:50:47.760 --> 00:50:51.022
So transgenerational empathy is understanding

1253
00:50:51.022 --> 00:50:53.430
what my dad had, why he had it,

1254
00:50:53.430 --> 00:50:57.030
why my dad harbored kind of anger towards his parents

1255
00:50:57.030 --> 00:50:58.980
for not leaving Europe, for not existing

1256
00:50:58.980 --> 00:51:02.130
and not living through it and making different decisions.

1257
00:51:02.130 --> 00:51:03.840
So it's understanding that that came

1258
00:51:03.840 --> 00:51:05.820
and that's at the individual layer.

1259
00:51:05.820 --> 00:51:09.570
And at the present moment,

1260
00:51:09.570 --> 00:51:12.060
it's about having self-compassion for yourself.

1261
00:51:12.060 --> 00:51:16.260
So empathy for yourself means, okay, I am who I am,

1262
00:51:16.260 --> 00:51:18.960
I'm doing the best that I can

1263
00:51:18.960 --> 00:51:21.150
as opposed to kind of constantly beating yourself up

1264
00:51:21.150 --> 00:51:21.983
against a metric and saying,

1265
00:51:21.983 --> 00:51:24.180
well, I could be that much better,

1266
00:51:24.180 --> 00:51:25.200
I could be that...

1267
00:51:25.200 --> 00:51:27.217
Because a lot of us are strivers,

1268
00:51:27.217 --> 00:51:28.100
like we're always,

1269
00:51:28.100 --> 00:51:31.800
the only yard stick we use is this imaginary one

1270
00:51:31.800 --> 00:51:33.480
that we somehow develop,

1271
00:51:33.480 --> 00:51:35.070
but we didn't somehow develop.

1272
00:51:35.070 --> 00:51:36.120
Just like the voice in our head

1273
00:51:36.120 --> 00:51:37.830
that came from something before us.

1274
00:51:37.830 --> 00:51:40.173
So it's about doing this kind of emotional,

1275
00:51:43.710 --> 00:51:45.390
it's almost like archeology.

1276
00:51:45.390 --> 00:51:46.890
Like going through the layers saying,

1277
00:51:46.890 --> 00:51:48.049
okay, that's who I am,

1278
00:51:48.049 --> 00:51:49.950
I'm doing the best I can.

1279
00:51:49.950 --> 00:51:54.540
Now what do I do to align myself to the present moment

1280
00:51:54.540 --> 00:51:58.530
and take action and change my behaviors,

1281
00:51:58.530 --> 00:52:01.170
not just for me right now and those around me,

1282
00:52:01.170 --> 00:52:03.270
but for those who are to come.

1283
00:52:03.270 --> 00:52:06.810
So transgenerational empathy is asking you to connect

1284
00:52:06.810 --> 00:52:09.000
in a true, authentic, emotional way

